===Natural Disasters===
====Earthquakes====
Okami is located near the boundary of three major tectonic plates—the Fosian Plate, the Shendan Plate, and the Mitoyagi Subplate. This complex geological intersection is responsible for the unique Y-shape of the Sanese Archipelago, tearing the northern landmass into the two distinct arms of Harujima and Ishijima from the base of Yagikoshu. The tectonic stress from this divergence created the Central Sanese Fault Zone. Just west of Okami, the Okami Thrust Fault actively pushed up the rugged western mountain range over millions of years, leaving the relatively flat coastal plains where the sprawling metropolis sits today. 

Because of this proximity to active fault lines, the Okami metropolitan area is an extremely active region for smaller quakes and slippage. It is not uncommon for the city to record hundreds of minor quakes (magnitudes 4 to 6) in a single year. These frequent events rarely cause damage but often result in a noticeable swaying sensation throughout the high-rises, a phenomenon that local residents merely brush off but which can be a significant source of anxiety for foreign visitors and Sanese citizens from less active regions. The epicenters for these frequent minor quakes are typically located offshore in the Shendan Ocean or deep within the uninhabited mountain ranges, as epicenters directly within the mainland urban area are quite rare.

Despite the high frequency of minor tremors, Okami rarely experiences direct, devastating megathrust earthquakes. The region has historically been struck by powerful seismic events in 1590, 1673, 1744, and most notably during the catastrophic Hokuso earthquake of 1821. The 1821 earthquake, with an estimated magnitude of 8.2, caused widespread destruction across the Okami plains and leveled much of the historical capital. This event marks the last time the urban center was directly hit by a major seismic disaster. 

Since 1821, the city has only experienced indirect effects from larger regional quakes, such as minor liquefaction in the artificial landfill zones along the bay during distant offshore ruptures in the 20th and 21st centuries. The long period of relative seismic calm directly under the city has allowed Okami to develop into a massive, uninterrupted urban center. However, the looming historical precedent has prompted the Sanese government to implement some of the strictest modern building codes in Adonia and mandate extensive disaster preparedness infrastructure to mitigate the inevitable threat of the next major rupture along the Okami Thrust Fault.

====Volcanic eruptions====
Although Okami rests on a seismically active plain, the city itself is not situated in the immediate shadow of an active stratovolcano like Mount Asuka on Ishijima. However, it is occasionally affected by distant ashfall from major eruptions in the Shinano Caldera or the southern mountain ranges of Harujima. To manage this threat, the Okami Meteorological Agency maintains a strict ash-monitoring protocol to prevent disruptions to the crucial aviation corridors at Okami-Tobu International Airport and the high-speed rail networks. Infrastructure across the metropolis, including the air filtration systems of the Okami Metro, is heavily reinforced to operate securely during prolonged periods of volcanic particulate dispersal.

====Floods====
Historically, the delta setting of the Kiso and Kashi rivers made early Okami highly susceptible to catastrophic flooding, particularly during the late summer typhoon season driven by storms from the Shendan Ocean. While the Honda clan's terraforming mitigated the worst of the estuarine surges in the pre-modern era, modern Okami faces the compounded threats of torrential rains and rising sea levels. To combat this, the Okami Metropolitan Government constructed a massive subterranean floodwater diversion facility beneath the northern wards. This colossal feat of civic engineering is heavily managed by the Satori algorithmic suite, which predicts surge levels in real time and automatically opens pressure valves to route excess water safely into the ocean without human intervention.

===Architecture===
====Early Modern (1403-1871)====
During the Honda Shogunate, the city was defined by strict architectural hierarchies and the sprawling use of local timber. The towering Okami Castle dominated the skyline, serving as both a military fortress and the administrative heart of the empire. Surrounding the castle in Nagashima were the sprawling, defensive estates of the samurai class, featuring fortified outer walls and manicured gardens. Conversely, the merchant class on Mugishima built densely packed wooden machiya townhouses that doubled as storefronts and residences. Because of this dense wooden construction, catastrophic fires were common, leading to the development of wide urban firebreaks and the iconic white-plastered storehouses designed to protect valuable mercantile goods from the flames.

====Modern (1871-1952)====
The Saito Restoration brought a sudden wave of Western architectural influence as the empire sought to project modern industrial strength on a global scale. The late 19th and early 20th centuries saw the rapid construction of monumental red-brick and stone edifices, heavily inspired by Brieslandic and Mercian styles. Key surviving examples of this era include the original preserved facade of Okami Central Station and the classical columns of the Bank of Okaiken. This era also introduced the first widespread use of reinforced concrete to the archipelago, though much of this early modern architecture was tragically lost during the incendiary firebombings of the Great Adonian War.

====Contemporary (1952-Present)====
Post-war Okami is a canvas of rapid, pragmatic rebuilding and avant-garde architectural experimentation. The 1960s and 1970s saw the rise of the domestic Metabolism movement, which envisioned buildings as living, adaptable organisms; the most famous surviving example is the massive Okami Tama Center with its striking inverted pyramid structures. Today, the skyline is dominated by sleek glass-and-steel supertall skyscrapers in the Minato and Aoi wards. Recent developments are uniquely characterized by deep integration with the state-operated Satori system, resulting in smart buildings that function as nodes within the city's broader algorithmic network to autonomously regulate energy usage, commuter flow, and climate controls.

==Administration==
{{main|Okami Metropolitan Government}}
===Local Government===
The Okami Metropolitan Government is responsible for the administration of the entire metropolis and functions as a unique hybrid of city and prefectural governance. The executive branch is headed by a directly elected Governor. The current Governor is Aono Michifumi, a self-described democratic socialist who has frequently clashed with the federal government over issues of public housing, infrastructure subsidies, and fiscal autonomy. The legislative branch is the Okami Metropolitan Assembly, which oversees a massive municipal budget that rivals the gross domestic product of many sovereign nations. This budget funds the city's extensive metropolitan police force, advanced fire and disaster response departments, and aggressive public housing initiatives.

The Metropolitan Assembly consists of 127 members elected from 18 electoral districts corresponding to the special wards. The assembly is responsible for enacting local ordinances, approving the metropolitan budget, and overseeing the administration of public services. The assembly's sessions are held at the Okami Metropolitan Government Building in Shinjuku, a distinctive building complex that has become an architectural landmark in its own right.

===National Government===
As the capital of Okaiken, Okami houses all three branches of the national government. The [[National Diet Building]], home to the bicameral legislature, is located in the Chiyoda district on Tenkyō-jima. The Diet consists of the [[House of Officers (Okaiken)|House of Officers]] (upper house, 251 members) and the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]] (lower house, 465 members). The Diet Building, completed in 1936, is an iconic structure featuring a distinctive pyramid-shaped central tower that has become a symbol of Sanese democracy.

The executive branch is centered on the Prime Minister's Official Residence (Kantei), also located in Chiyoda. The Kantei, a modern structure completed in 2002, houses the offices of the Prime Minister and the Cabinet. The Prime Minister also maintains an official residence nearby. The various ministries of the national government are concentrated in the Kasumigaseki district, forming a bureaucratic complex known as "Kasumigaseki Hill" that employs thousands of civil servants.

The [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]] is located in the Hayabusacho district, near the Diet Building. The court's distinctive modern building houses the fifteen justices who serve as the final arbiters of constitutional interpretation in Okaiken.

The [[Imperial Palace]], the primary residence of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]], occupies the site of the former Okami Castle in the heart of the city. The palace grounds cover approximately 2.3 square kilometers and include the main palace buildings, the Imperial Household Agency, the Privy Council chambers, and extensive gardens. While most of the palace is closed to the public, the East Gardens are open to visitors year-round.

