===Special Wards===
{{main|Special wards of Okami}}
The 18 special wards of Okami are the primary administrative divisions of the former city of Okami. Each ward is a local municipality with its own elected mayor and assembly, functioning essentially as individual cities while remaining part of the larger Okami Metropolis. The special wards cover an area of approximately 621 km² and have a combined population of over 8.2 million.

The special wards are:
* [[Aoi Ward, Okami|Aoi]] – Financial district and corporate headquarters
* [[Chuo Ward, Okami|Chuo]] – Commercial center and historic merchant district
* [[Higashi Ward, Okami|Higashi]] – Eastern residential district
* [[Kanda Ward, Okami|Kanda]] – Education and research hub
* [[Kita Ward, Okami|Kita]] – Northern industrial zone
* [[Minami Ward, Okami|Minami]] – Southern commercial district
* [[Minato Ward, Okami|Minato]] – Port and waterfront development zone
* [[Nagata Ward, Okami|Nagata]] – Government district
* [[Nakano Ward, Okami|Nakano]] – Cultural district
* [[Nishi Ward, Okami|Nishi]] – Western residential district
* [[Sakura Ward, Okami|Sakura]] – Cherry blossom viewing area
* [[Shibuya Ward, Okami|Shibuya]] – Entertainment and fashion center
* [[Shinagawa Ward, Okami|Shinagawa]] – High-tech industrial zone
* [[Shinjuku Ward, Okami|Shinjuku]] – Administrative center and nightlife district
* [[Sugamo Ward, Okami|Sugamo]] – Traditional shopping district
* [[Tama Ward, Okami|Tama]] – Modern commercial development
* [[Tatara Ward, Okami|Tatara]] – Heavy industrial and shipbuilding zone
* [[Ueno Ward, Okami|Ueno]] – Parks and cultural institutions

==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of Okami}}
Okami is the economic powerhouse of Okaiken and one of the major financial centers of Adonia. The Greater Okami metropolitan area accounts for approximately one-third of the nation's GDP and serves as the headquarters for the majority of Okaiken's largest corporations, financial institutions, and international organizations.

===Financial Services===
The city's financial district, centered on the Aoi and Chuo wards, is home to the [[Okami Stock Exchange]], one of the largest stock exchanges in Adonia by market capitalization. The exchange lists over 3,500 companies and plays a crucial role in the nation's capital markets. Major financial institutions headquartered in Okami include the [[Bank of Okaiken]], the [[Sanese Development Bank]], and the nation's largest commercial banks and insurance companies.

The Okami International Financial Center, located in the Minato waterfront development zone, has emerged as a major hub for international finance, attracting regional headquarters of multinational corporations and serving as a gateway for foreign investment into Okaiken.

===Technology and Innovation===
Okami has established itself as a leading center for technology and innovation. The city is home to the [[Satori Development Institute]], the nation's primary research facility for artificial intelligence and algorithmic governance. The Kanda district houses numerous technology startups and research laboratories, while the Shinagawa ward hosts major electronics manufacturers and telecommunications companies.

The city's commitment to smart city development has made it a global leader in urban technology. The integration of the Satori algorithmic system into city infrastructure has enabled the development of autonomous transportation networks, smart grid energy systems, and AI-powered public services.

===Industry===
While the city's economy has shifted toward services, Okami retains significant industrial capacity. The Tatara ward houses the [[Okami Industrial Zone]], one of the nation's largest concentrations of heavy industry. The zone includes major shipyards, steel mills, and chemical processing facilities. The historic shipyards of Tatara have produced vessels for the Sanese navy and merchant marine for centuries.

The light manufacturing sector is concentrated in the Kita and Shinagawa wards, producing electronics, precision instruments, and consumer goods. The city's printing and publishing industry, centered on the traditional paper-making districts, remains significant and reflects the city's historical identity as "Big Paper."

===Tourism and Hospitality===
Okami attracts millions of domestic and international visitors annually, making tourism a significant sector of the economy. The city's cultural attractions, including historic temples, museums, and entertainment districts, draw visitors from across Adonia. The hotel and hospitality industry is concentrated in the central wards, with luxury accommodations in Shinjuku and Shibuya and business hotels throughout the commercial districts.

==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Okami}}
Okami's population of over 8.2 million in the special wards and 32 million in the metropolitan area makes it the most populous urban area in Okaiken and one of the largest in Adonia. The city's population density of approximately 4,400 per km² in the special wards is among the highest in the nation, though lower than some smaller urban centers.

The city has experienced steady population growth throughout the post-war period, though growth rates have slowed in recent decades as the metropolitan area has matured. The population of the special wards peaked at approximately 8.9 million in 2015 before declining slightly, reflecting broader demographic trends toward suburbanization and an aging population.

===Ethnic Composition===
Okami is home to the largest concentration of foreign residents in Okaiken. As of 2017, approximately 500,000 foreign nationals resided in the city, representing over 6% of the population. The largest foreign communities are from [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]], [[Dagit]], and [[Yanbaru]], with smaller communities from [[Nakamizu]] and various Illypnian nations.

The city's historic [[Zong Town]] in the Chuo ward remains a vibrant ethnic enclave, while more recent immigrant communities have established themselves in the Tatara and Minami wards. The city government has implemented various programs to support foreign residents, including multilingual services and cultural integration initiatives.

===Religion===
Okami's religious landscape reflects the broader Sanese pattern of syncretic practice combining [[Shoto]] and Buddhist traditions. The city is home to numerous major shrines and temples, including the historic [[Kofukuji Temple]] where Ogasawara Terumasa spent his final night, and the [[Okami Grand Shrine]], one of the most important Shoto shrines in the nation.

The Imperial Palace's sacred grounds house the [[Sanshu no Jingi]], the three sacred treasures of the Imperial regalia, making the city the spiritual as well as political center of the nation. Religious observance remains significant in city life, with major festivals drawing millions of participants annually.

==Education==
{{Main|Education in Okami}}
Okami is the educational center of Okaiken, home to the nation's most prestigious universities and research institutions. The city's concentration of higher education institutions attracts students from across the nation and internationally.

===Universities and Higher Education===
The [[University of Okami]], founded in 1877, is the nation's oldest and most prestigious university. The university's main campus is located in the Hongo district, with additional campuses throughout the metropolitan area. The university is consistently ranked among the top institutions in Adonia and has produced numerous Nobel laureates and national leaders.

Other major universities include [[Okami Institute of Technology]], known for engineering and applied sciences; [[Keio University]], a prestigious private institution; [[Waseda University]], known for its programs in law and political science; and [[Sophia University]], a private university with strong international programs.

The city is also home to numerous specialized institutions, including the [[National Defense Academy]], the [[Imperial Music Academy]], and the [[Okami University of the Arts]]. Medical education is concentrated at the [[Okami University School of Medicine]] and several teaching hospitals throughout the city.

===Primary and Secondary Education===
Primary and secondary education in Okami is administered by the Okami Metropolitan Government. The city operates over 2,000 public elementary and junior high schools and approximately 300 public high schools. In addition, numerous private schools provide alternative educational options, including prestigious preparatory schools that feed into the nation's top universities.

==Culture==
{{Main|Culture of Okami}}
Okami's cultural influence extends throughout Okaiken and beyond. The city has been the center of Sanese culture for over four centuries, and its arts, cuisine, and fashion trends influence the entire nation.

===Cuisine===
Okami cuisine reflects the city's history as a center of trade and political power. The city is famous for its distinctive style of sushi, developed in the Edo period when fresh fish was delivered to the capital via fast boats. Other notable dishes include ''Okami ramen'', a soy-based noodle soup developed in the post-war period, and ''monjayaki'', a savory pancake popular in the Tsukishima district.

The city's restaurant scene is among the most diverse in Adonia, with establishments ranging from centuries-old traditional restaurants to cutting-edge molecular gastronomy. The Tsukiji Outer Market remains a major culinary attraction, offering fresh seafood and traditional street foods.

===Festivals===
Okami hosts numerous festivals throughout the year, drawing participants and spectators from across the nation. The [[Okami Summer Festival]], held in July and August, includes hundreds of events throughout the city, culminating in massive fireworks displays over the Hiryū River. The [[Cherry Blossom Festival]] in March and April attracts millions of visitors to the city's parks and gardens.

The [[Festival of the Lanterns]], held in the historic Tamashima district each autumn, preserves traditions dating to the Edo period. The festival features traditional music, dance, and elaborate lantern displays that illuminate the narrow streets of the entertainment district.

===Museums and Cultural Institutions===
Okami is home to the nation's premier museums and cultural institutions. The [[Okami National Museum]] houses the world's largest collection of Sanese art and artifacts. The [[National Museum of Modern Art]] showcases the development of modern Sanese art. The [[Okami Metropolitan Art Museum]] hosts major international exhibitions.

The city's performing arts scene is centered on the [[Okami Metropolitan Opera House]] and the [[National Theater of Okaiken]]. Traditional performing arts, including ''kabuki'', ''noh'', and ''bunraku'', are performed at historic venues including the [[Kabuki-za Theater]] in the Ginza district.

===Sports===
{{Main|Sport in Okaiken}}
Okami's sports culture reflects the national passion for both traditional and modern athletic pursuits. The city is home to the [[Okami Giants]], one of the nation's most popular professional baseball teams, and hosts numerous professional football, basketball, and sumo tournaments throughout the year.

The city has a particular reputation for the "Cult of Iron" sports culture that emerged in the Tatara ward, emphasizing intense physical conditioning and martial arts. The [[Tatara Sports Complex]] has produced numerous national and international champions in boxing, wrestling, and mixed martial arts.

The sport of ''sumo'' is closely associated with Okami, with the [[Kokugikan Arena]] in the Ryogoku district hosting three of the six annual grand tournaments. The [[Okami Marathon]], held each February, attracts elite runners from around the world.

==Transportation==
{{main|Transportation in Okami|Okami Metro}}
Okami is the central logistical hub of Okaiken's domestic and international transit networks. The city is served by the Okami Metro, an expansive rapid transit network comprising 12 lines and over 140 stations, which moves millions of commuters daily. Following the privatization of the system in 2018, the network has become heavily reliant on the Satori algorithm to optimize train frequencies and manage crowds during peak industrial shift changes.

For national travel, Okami Central Station serves as the primary terminus for the high-speed Harujima Shinkansen and the broader Okaiken Railways (OR Harujima) commuter network, connecting the capital directly to major cities like Toyozawa and Shinagawa. International and domestic aviation is handled primarily by Okami-Tobu International Airport, which serves as the flagship hub for the national carrier, Okaiken Air, maintaining critical trans-Althenian corridors for the Sanese economy.

===Air Transportation===
[[Okami-Tobu International Airport]] is the primary international gateway to Okaiken, handling approximately 45 million passengers annually. The airport serves as the main hub for [[Okaiken Air]] and [[Sanesair]], the nation's flag carriers. A second airport, [[Okami-Seibu International Airport]] (Asajima), primarily handles domestic flights and serves as a secondary hub for regional carriers.

===Rail Transportation===
The Okami Metro subway system is one of the most extensive and heavily used urban transit networks in the world. The system's 12 lines carry approximately 8 million passengers daily. The network is complemented by the [[Okami Loop Line]], a circular rail line that connects the major urban centers, and numerous commuter rail lines operated by Okaiken Railways and private companies.

[[Okami Central Station]] is the nation's busiest rail terminal, serving as the terminus for the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] and the hub for intercity and commuter rail services. The station complex, rebuilt after the war, handles over 400,000 passengers daily.

===Road Transportation===
Okami is connected to the national [[expressway]] network via several major routes. The [[Okami Expressway]] circles the city center, while the [[Harujima Expressway]] connects the capital with the northern prefectures. Traffic congestion remains a significant challenge despite extensive infrastructure development, leading to increasing reliance on public transportation.

===Port Facilities===
The [[Port of Okami]] is one of the nation's largest commercial ports, handling container traffic, bulk cargo, and passenger ferries. The port facilities have been extensively modernized since the post-war reconstruction and now include some of the most automated cargo handling facilities in Adonia.

==International Relations==
===Diplomatic Missions===
As the national capital, Okami hosts the embassies and consulates of all nations maintaining diplomatic relations with Okaiken. The [[Kasumigaseki Diplomatic District]] houses the majority of foreign embassies, while honorary consulates are located throughout the metropolitan area. The city also hosts the regional headquarters of numerous international organizations.

===Sister Cities===
Okami maintains sister city relationships with major cities around the world, promoting cultural exchange and economic cooperation. Major sister city relationships include:
* [[New Valencia]], [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]] (since 1960)
* [[Akyatan]], [[Dagit]] (since 1975)
* [[Yomitan]], [[Yanbaru]] (since 1985)
* [[Fuzhou]], [[Kingdom of Zong]] (since 1992)

==Notable People==
{{Main|List of people from Okami}}
Okami has been home to numerous significant figures throughout Okaiken's history. Historical figures include the Honda shoguns who ruled from the city for over 250 years, and the statesmen of the Saito Restoration who transformed Okaiken into a modern nation. The city has produced countless artists, writers, and intellectuals who have shaped Sanese culture.

Notable modern figures include [[Honda Shigetsuma]], the current Prime Minister of Okaiken; [[Aono Michifumi]], the current Governor of Okami; numerous business leaders who head the nation's largest corporations; and artists and entertainers who have achieved international recognition.

